GROWTH GAPS

1. High-Stakes Hooking and Storytelling Serialization
Creators like @dhavalkataria_ (20.28% engagement rate) leverage challenge-driven series formats like "UN-BROKE-ING INDIA" or dramatic hook lines ("You will never make 1 crore in life..."). Meanwhile, @garvit.irl opens posts with straightforward tool descriptions ("ChatGPT's Dots, Muse, and Grokbot, they all look the same..."). Framing AI tutorials inside a story or challenge format creates stronger retention and virality.

2. Direct Comment Ingestion and Save-Driven Workflows
@favourite.engineer drives high audience action by offering copy-paste prompts triggered via comments ("Comment prompt to get it"). Garvit explains technical concepts (like Claude skills/markdown files) effectively, but misses explicit comment triggers and step-by-step visual UI prompts that maximize saved posts and comment section engagement.

3. News-Style Highlighting of Breaking AI Workflows
@nick_saraev and @thevarunmayya frame tool releases as major industry shifts or zero-cost business transformations ("Run a whole agency inside Claude Code for $0"). Garvit focuses heavily on tactical feature comparisons (e.g., 11labs alternatives), missing out on broader business outcome hooks that resonate with non-developer audiences.


RECOMMENDED ACTIONS

1. Transition 100% of Feed Content to Reels
Reels average 200 likes per post compared to static/carousel posts which average 31 likes for @garvit.irl. Discontinue single image/carousel posts and publish strictly short-form video Reels during the peak engagement window of 6:00 PM - 9:00 PM IST.

2. Introduce a Named 3-Part Challenge Series
Launch an ongoing challenge series (e.g., "Automating a Business in 7 Days with AI"). Use storytelling hooks in the first 3 seconds to establish real stakes, modeling the successful format of @dhavalkataria_.

3. Add CTA Automation Keywords in Script Hooks
Include explicit comment-driven calls to action at the end of technical videos (e.g., "Comment CLAUDE to get my exact 5 markdown system prompts"). This leverages Garvit's strong baseline of 128 comments per post to push content further into algorithmic recommendation feeds.
